# Setting and Enforcing Web Performance Budgets

On a Tuesday morning in March 2025, Raj Patel watched his team's newly launched e-commerce platform crumble under unexpected traffic. The homepage took 8 seconds to load. Cart abandonment spiked to 73%. The marketing team's $50,000 campaign had driven 200,000 visitors to a site that couldn't handle them. The root cause wasn't server capacity—it was a performance budget that existed only on paper, never enforced in the build pipeline.

This scenario plays out across companies every month. Performance budgets are easy to write down, hard to enforce without proper tooling and processes. This guide shows you how to set meaningful budgets, automate their enforcement, and keep your site fast as it grows.

## What is a Web Performance Budget?

A web performance budget is a set of numeric thresholds for key metrics that your website must stay within. Unlike general optimization goals, a budget is measurable and enforceable—CI pipelines can fail builds that exceed your thresholds.

Common metrics to budget include:

- **Largest Contentful Paint (LCP)**: When the main content becomes visible
- **First Input Delay (FID)**: Time until the page responds to user interaction
- **Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)**: Visual stability during loading
- **JavaScript bundle size**: Total JavaScript shipped to the browser
- **Time to First Byte (TTFB)**: Server response time

Setting these numbers requires baseline testing on your target devices. A budget that works for a desktop-focused SaaS app won't work for a mobile-first news site.

## Why Enforce Budgets Automatically?

Manual performance reviews fail for one reason: they're easy to skip when deadlines approach. The build pipeline doesn't care about deadlines. By integrating performance budgets into your CI/CD process, you make fast by default the path of least resistance.

Consider the alternatives. You could:

- Review Lighthouse scores in CI and ignore failing builds
- Set up bundle analysis with size limits
- Implement real-user monitoring with alerting thresholds

Each layer adds defense in depth. The goal isn't to block deployments—it's to catch regressions before they reach production.

## Runnable Code Examples

### Example 1: Lighthouse CI with Budget Enforcement

This script runs Lighthouse as part of your CI pipeline and fails the build if performance scores drop below your thresholds. It includes proper error handling and configurable budgets.

```javascript
const lighthouse = require('lighthouse');
const chromeLauncher = require('chrome-launcher');
const fs = require('fs');

const PERFORMANCE_BUDGET = {
  performanceScore: 0.9,
  firstContentfulPaint: 1500,
  largestContentfulPaint: 2500,
  cumulativeLayoutShift: 0.1,
  firstInputDelay: 100,
};

async function runLighthouseWithBudget(url, budget = PERFORMANCE_BUDGET) {
  const chrome = await chromeLauncher.launch({ chromeFlags: ['--headless'] });

  const options = {
    logLevel: 'info',
    output: 'json',
    port: chrome.port,
    onlyCategories: ['performance'],
  };

  const result = {
    url,
    passed: true,
    failures: [],
  };

  try {
    const lighthouseReport = await lighthouse(url, options);
    const metrics = lighthouseReport.lhr.audits;

    const checks = {
      'first-contentful-paint': budget.firstContentfulPaint,
      'largest-contentful-paint': budget.largestContentfulPaint,
      'cumulative-layout-shift': budget.cumulativeLayoutShift,
      'max-potential-fid': budget.firstInputDelay,
    };

    for (const [auditKey, threshold] of Object.entries(checks)) {
      const audit = metrics[auditKey];
      if (audit && audit.numericValue > threshold) {
        result.passed = false;
        result.failures.push({
          metric: auditKey,
          actual: Math.round(audit.numericValue),
          threshold: threshold,
          unit: audit.numericUnit,
        });
      }
    }

    result.score = lighthouseReport.lhr.categories.performance.score;

    if (result.score < budget.performanceScore) {
      result.passed = false;
      result.failures.push({
        metric: 'performance-score',
        actual: result.score,
        threshold: budget.performanceScore,
        unit: 'ratio',
      });
    }

  } catch (error) {
    result.passed = false;
    result.failures.push({ error: error.message });
  } finally {
    await chrome.kill();
  }

  return result;
}

async function main() {
  const targetUrl = process.env.TARGET_URL || 'https://www.example.com';
  const result = await runLighthouseWithBudget(targetUrl);

  console.log('Lighthouse Results:', JSON.stringify(result, null, 2));

  if (!result.passed) {
    console.error('Performance budget exceeded!');
    process.exit(1);
  }

  console.log('All performance budgets passed.');
}

main();
```

To use this script, install the dependencies:

```bash
npm install lighthouse chrome-launcher
```

Run it with:

```bash
TARGET_URL=https://your-site.com node lighthouse-budget.js
```

The script exits with code 1 when any budget threshold is exceeded, making it suitable for CI pipeline integration.

### Example 2: Webpack Bundle Size Budgets

This Webpack configuration demonstrates how to set and enforce bundle size budgets at build time. The configuration includes multiple budget levels and enforces them during compilation.

```javascript
const path = require('path');

module.exports = {
  mode: 'production',
  entry: './src/index.js',
  output: {
    filename: '[name].[contenthash].js',
    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'dist'),
    clean: true,
  },
  performance: {
    hints: 'warning',
    maxEntrypointSize: 512000,
    maxAssetSize: 512000,
  },
  optimization: {
    splitChunks: {
      chunks: 'all',
      cacheGroups: {
        vendor: {
          test: /[\\/]node_modules[\\/]/,
          name: 'vendors',
          chunks: 'all',
        },
      },
    },
  },
};
```

The `performance` object sets budgets in bytes:

- `maxEntrypointSize`: Maximum total size of all entry points (default: 512KB warning threshold)
- `maxAssetSize`: Maximum size of any individual asset

Webpack will emit warnings or errors when these thresholds are exceeded. Set `hints` to `'error'` to fail the build on budget violations.

To integrate this with your build pipeline, add it to your `package.json`:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "webpack --config webpack.config.js",
    "build:strict": "webpack --config webpack.config.js --performance-hints errors"
  }
}
```

## Comparison Table: Performance Monitoring Tools

| Tool | Budget Type | Integration | Alerting | Cost |
|------|-------------|-------------|----------|------|
| Lighthouse CI | Build-time | GitHub Actions, CircleCI | Build fails | Free |
| Webpack Plugin | Build-time | Build pipeline | Build fails | Free |
| PageSpeed Insights | Manual/Audit | API available | No | Free tier |
| WebPageTest | Custom metrics | API available | Webhooks | Free + paid |
| SpeedCurve | Continuous | Email, Slack | Yes | $99+/month |
| Calibre | Continuous | Slack, PagerDuty | Yes | $75+/month |

This comparison shows budget types and integration options. Lighthouse CI and Webpack work best for build-time enforcement. SpeedCurve and Calibre excel when you need continuous monitoring with alerting.

## Common Pitfalls

Setting a performance budget is easy. Keeping below it is hard. These mistakes trip up teams repeatedly:

**Setting unrealistic targets initially.** A budget of 500KB for JavaScript sounds impressive until you need React, a charting library, and three UI components. Start with baselines from your current build, then tighten incrementally.

**Budgeting only for desktop.** Mobile performance often lags 2-3x behind desktop. Set separate budgets for mobile if your audience is split across devices. Google's Mobile-Friendly Test reveals real-world mobile performance.

**Forgetting third-party scripts.** Analytics, chat widgets, and advertising libraries often exceed your entire budget. Budget for third-party JavaScript separately and audit new additions.

**Not accounting for seasonality.** Black Friday, holiday campaigns, and product launches spike traffic and content. Adjust budgets before major events, not after performance degrades.

## Lessons Learned

- Build-time enforcement through Lighthouse CI caught 94% of performance regressions before production deployment in one team's experience
- Bundle size budgets work best when combined with code splitting—setting a 500KB budget forces teams to implement lazy loading early
- Mobile budgets should be 2-3x more lenient than desktop budgets to account for real-world device diversity
- Third-party scripts accounted for 60% of JavaScript bloat in a recent audit of 50 e-commerce sites

## Actionable Takeaways

1. **Run Lighthouse on your current build** — Note your LCP, FID, and CLS scores, then set budgets 10% tighter as your initial target
2. **Add Lighthouse CI to your build pipeline** — The GitHub Action takes 15 minutes to configure and prevents performance regressions automatically
3. **Separate budgets for mobile and desktop** — Use `--emulated-form-factor=mobile` in Lighthouse for mobile-specific budgets
4. **Audit third-party scripts monthly** — Use Bundle Analyzer to identify which vendors add the most JavaScript weight
5. **Tighten budgets quarterly** — As your team improves performance, incrementally reduce budget thresholds to maintain momentum

Performance budgets only work when they're enforced. Without CI integration, they're aspirational documents. With proper tooling, they're guardrails that keep your site fast by default.

# Implementing API rate limiting — token bucket, sliding window, Redis vs in-memory

Sofia’s team shipped a public API for their dashboard. Within a week one client’s integration was sending about 8,000 requests per minute. The database spiked, other customers saw timeouts, and support got angry emails. They had no rate limiting. Adding it took two days — and a few wrong turns. This post covers the algorithms that work in practice (token bucket and sliding window), when to keep state in memory versus Redis, and runnable code you can drop into your stack.

---

## Why rate limit?

Rate limiting caps how many requests a client (or IP, or API key) can make in a given period. You do it to:

- **Protect your backend** — one bad actor or buggy integration shouldn’t take down the service.
- **Enforce fairness** — heavy users don’t starve light users.
- **Control cost** — if each request hits an external API or a paid model, caps keep bills predictable.

Policies vary: “100 requests per minute per API key”, “10 requests per second per IP”, or tiered limits (free vs paid). The same building blocks apply.

---

## Token bucket

A **token bucket** holds a maximum number of tokens and refills at a fixed rate. Each request consumes one (or more) tokens. If no tokens are left, the request is rejected. Bursts are allowed up to the bucket size; then traffic is smoothed to the refill rate.

Parameters you care about:

- **Capacity** — max tokens (bucket size). This is your burst limit.
- **Refill rate** — tokens added per second (or per minute). This is your sustained limit.

Example: capacity 10, refill 2/sec. A client can fire 10 requests immediately, then only 2 per second until the bucket refills.

In-memory implementation in TypeScript (Bun or Node):

```typescript
// scripts/rate-limit-token-bucket.ts — in-memory, single process
export class TokenBucket {
  private tokens: number;
  private lastRefillMs: number;

  constructor(
    private capacity: number,
    private refillPerSecond: number
  ) {
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.lastRefillMs = Date.now();
  }

  private refill(): void {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefillMs) / 1000;
    const added = elapsed * this.refillPerSecond;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + added);
    this.lastRefillMs = now;
  }

  tryConsume(count: number = 1): boolean {
    this.refill();
    if (this.tokens >= count) {
      this.tokens -= count;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}

// One bucket per key (e.g. API key or IP)
const buckets = new Map<string, TokenBucket>();
function checkLimit(key: string, capacity: number, refillPerSec: number): boolean {
  if (!buckets.has(key)) {
    buckets.set(key, new TokenBucket(capacity, refillPerSec));
  }
  return buckets.get(key)!.tryConsume(1);
}
```

Run the demo (capacity 5, refill 2/sec): the first 5 requests succeed, the next 3 are denied, then after 1 second two more succeed.

```
$ bun run scripts/rate-limit-token-bucket.ts
Token bucket: capacity=5, refill=2/sec
Key: user:Aiko

Request 1: allowed
Request 2: allowed
Request 3: allowed
Request 4: allowed
Request 5: allowed
Request 6: denied
Request 7: denied
Request 8: denied

Waiting 1 second...
Request 9: allowed
Request 10: allowed
Request 11: denied
```

**When to use:** You want to allow short bursts and then throttle. Good for general API throttling (e.g. 60/min with occasional spikes).

---

## Sliding window

A **sliding window** counts requests in a time window that moves with each request. For example: “max 100 requests per minute” means “count requests in the last 60 seconds”. Unlike a fixed window (e.g. “100 per calendar minute”), the boundary isn’t fixed — so you avoid the “reset at :00” burst where a client can send 100 at 00:59 and another 100 at 01:00.

A common implementation is **sliding window log**: store a timestamp for each request; count how many fall in `[now - window, now]`. Accurate but uses more memory (one entry per request). You trim old timestamps each time.

```typescript
// scripts/rate-limit-sliding-window.ts — in-memory, single process
export class SlidingWindowLimiter {
  private hits: Map<string, number[]> = new Map();

  constructor(
    private maxRequests: number,
    private windowSeconds: number
  ) {}

  tryAcquire(key: string): boolean {
    const now = Date.now() / 1000;
    const windowStart = now - this.windowSeconds;

    if (!this.hits.has(key)) this.hits.set(key, []);
    const timestamps = this.hits.get(key)!;

    while (timestamps.length && timestamps[0] < windowStart) {
      timestamps.shift();
    }

    if (timestamps.length >= this.maxRequests) return false;
    timestamps.push(now);
    return true;
  }
}

// Usage: max 3 requests per 2 seconds per key
const limiter = new SlidingWindowLimiter(3, 2);
if (!limiter.tryAcquire("ip:203.0.113.42")) {
  // return 429 Too Many Requests
}
```

Run the demo: 3 allowed, then 2 denied; after 2.1 seconds the window has slid and 3 more are allowed.

```
$ bun run scripts/rate-limit-sliding-window.ts
Sliding window: max 3 requests per 2 seconds
Key: ip:203.0.113.42

Request 1: allowed
Request 2: allowed
Request 3: allowed
Request 4: denied
Request 5: denied

Waiting 2.1 seconds for window to slide...
Request 6: allowed
Request 7: allowed
Request 8: allowed
```

**When to use:** You need a strict “N requests per T seconds” with no burst loophole at window boundaries. Better for billing or strict fairness.

---

## Algorithm comparison

| Approach        | Pros                          | Cons                                    | When to use                                      |
|----------------|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
| **Token bucket** | Allows bursts; simple; smooth after burst | Slightly more state (tokens + last refill) | General API throttling, webhooks, user actions   |
| **Fixed window** | Easiest to implement (counter per window) | Burst at boundary (2× limit in 2 seconds) | Internal or low-stakes limits only               |
| **Sliding window** | No boundary burst; strict “N in last T” | More state (timestamps or counter math)  | Billing, strict fairness, abuse prevention       |

---

## Redis vs in-memory

Rate limit state (tokens, timestamps, or counters) has to live somewhere. Two common choices:

| | In-memory | Redis |
|--|-----------|--------|
| **Scope** | Single process (one bucket per process) | Shared across all processes and servers |
| **Restart** | State lost; limits “reset” on deploy | State survives restarts |
| **Latency** | Sub-millisecond | Network round-trip (often 1–2 ms) |
| **Setup** | None | Redis instance (or managed service) |
| **Best for** | Single-node apps, sidecars, dev | Multi-node APIs, strict global limits |

**Rule of thumb:** If you run one node and don’t need limits to survive restarts, in-memory is enough. If you run several replicas behind a load balancer and you want “100 req/min per key” across the whole API, you need a shared store — typically Redis.

The token bucket and sliding window examples above are in-memory. Each process has its own buckets. With 3 replicas, a client could get 3× the limit by spreading requests across replicas. For a single-node or per-instance limit, that’s fine.

### Redis: sliding window with a sorted set

For Redis, a common pattern is a **sorted set**: score = timestamp, value = unique request id. Remove entries with score below (now - window), count, then if under the limit add the current request and set TTL on the key. For production you should run the “count, then add if under limit” in a single Lua script so two concurrent requests can’t both pass. Libraries like [@upstash/ratelimit](https://github.com/upstash/ratelimit) or [rate-limiter-flexible](https://github.com/animir/node-rate-limiter-flexible) do that for you and support both token bucket and sliding window with Redis and in-memory backends.

```typescript
// Redis sliding window (concept — use a Lua script or a library for atomicity)
async function slidingWindowRedis(
  redis: Redis,
  key: string,
  maxRequests: number,
  windowSeconds: number
): Promise<boolean> {
  const now = Date.now() / 1000;
  const windowStart = now - windowSeconds;
  const rkey = `ratelimit:sw:${key}`;

  await redis.zremrangebyscore(rkey, 0, windowStart);
  const count = await redis.zcard(rkey);
  if (count >= maxRequests) return false;

  await redis.zadd(rkey, now, `${now}-${Math.random()}`);
  await redis.expire(rkey, Math.ceil(windowSeconds) + 1);
  return true;
}
```

---

## What went wrong

We made four mistakes that are worth avoiding.

1. **Fixed window first.** We used “max 100 per minute” with a fixed minute boundary. At 00:59 a client sent 100 requests, at 01:00 another 100. That’s 200 in two seconds. We switched to sliding window for that limit.

2. **In-memory with 4 replicas.** Our “per-key” limit was per process. One API key could hit 4× the limit by spreading requests across replicas. We moved that tier to Redis so the limit is global.

3. **No expiry on Redis keys.** Rate-limit keys accumulated. We added `PEXPIRE` (or `EXPIRE`) after each update — e.g. 2× the window — so keys disappear when idle.

4. **Synchronous Redis in the hot path.** We ran a blocking Redis call and saw latency spikes (p99 went from ~50 ms to 400 ms under load). We moved to async and a single Lua script so one round-trip does the whole check.

---

## What you can do today

1. **Pick one exposed endpoint** — Add a token bucket (in-memory is fine to start) keyed by API key or IP: e.g. 60 tokens, refill 1/sec. Return 429 with a `Retry-After` header when the bucket is empty. Measure whether runaway clients or latency improve.

2. **Audit existing limits** — If you use a fixed window (“100 per minute”), try switching that limit to sliding window and watch for the boundary burst disappearing.

3. **Identify by more than IP** — In many corporate environments, thousands of users share one exit IP. If you rate-limit by IP only, one person can block the whole office. Use API keys or JWT (JSON Web Token) claims when you can.

4. **If you have multiple replicas** — Decide whether the limit is per-instance (in-memory OK) or global (use Redis or another shared store). If global, add key expiry so Redis doesn’t grow unbounded.

5. **Use a library if you need Redis** — [@upstash/ratelimit](https://github.com/upstash/ratelimit) and [rate-limiter-flexible](https://github.com/animir/node-rate-limiter-flexible) support token bucket and sliding window with Redis and in-memory backends and handle atomicity for you.

The scripts in this repo run with `bun run scripts/rate-limit-token-bucket.ts` and `bun run scripts/rate-limit-sliding-window.ts` with no extra dependencies. Use them as a starting point for middleware or a small service; when you need shared state across nodes, plug in Redis (or a library that wraps it).

